  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy/areas of frost possible across northern \nareas tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region tonight, bringing \nclear skies and light winds. This will result in favorable \nconditions for radiational cooling. Low temperatures will \ngenerally range from the mid to upper 30s across much of \nnorthern Maine. However, temperatures could locally drop to \naround freezing across northwest areas. Patchy/areas of frost \nare possible tonight across northern areas, particularly in the\nnormally colder low lying locations. Patchy river valley fog is\nalso possible later tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chance for rain showers by late next week,\nthough little to no drought improvement anticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system to the far north moves eastward, pushing a\nfront through New England late next week. Ensemble models have\nslowed the progression of the front with more members leans\ntowards Thursday evening and Thursday night for rain. Upper air\nmodel soundings indicate a large dry airmass ahead of the front.\nThis coupled with the decreasing intensity of the front, rain\nshowers look to be minimal, especially for the north. Cannot\nrule out some scattered rain showers across the south.",
